        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The 30 Day Head Down Tilt (HDT30) study was conducted at the Flight Analogs Research Unit (FARU) located at the University of Texas Medical Branch (UTMB) in Galveston, Texas.  The study began on October 13, 2009, and ended on May 13, 2010.  The study was active for 252 calendar days and completed a total of 705 study days (the latter number includes all enrolled subjects. The subjects were recruited, screened, and approved for participation according to the documented Inclusion/Exclusion Criteria.  Once enrolled, the subjects were placed in a bed rest facility for testing and data collection during 13 days of ambulatory acclimation, 30 days of 6-degree head down tilt bed rest, and 14 days of ambulatory recovery. A total of 13 subjects participated in the study, of those, only 12 subjects completed this study and one was dismissed prior to starting the head down phase.  Males accounted for 8 of the completed subjects and females accounted for the other four.  <P>
HDT30 follows the Flight Analog Project (FAP) guidelines for standard conditions of bed rest, and FAP Standard Measures collection, with modifications to achieve inter-study compatibility and facilitate the implementation of individual research protocol objectives. The standard measures were integrated with the peer-reviewed science requirements on a noninterference basis. The Standard Measures protocols used by FAP were previously reviewed and approved by the JSC Committee for the Protection of Human Subjects (CPHS) and the UTMB Institutional Review Board (IRB). <P>
<i>Bed Rest Standard Conditions Overview </I><BR>
<OL>
<LI> The room temperature (72 ± 2 ºF) and humidity (70 ± 5%) was maintained by FARU.
<LI> Subject light/dark cycle was strictly enforced. Subjects were awoken at 06:00 and room lights were extinguished at 22:00 (military time) 7 d/wk. Napping was not allowed.
<LI> Pre-bed rest (ambulatory) phase of 11 to 15 days
<LI> Bed rest phase of up to 30 days at 6 degrees head-down tilt
<LI> Post-bed rest (recovery) phase of 14 days
<LI> Daily 24-hour urine collection
<LI> Daily iron supplementation for female subjects
<LI> Daily vitamin D supplementation during the in-bed phase. Supplementation was administered daily during the pre-bed rest phase if the subject had a low vitamin D level during screening. 
<LI> Colace was given daily during bed rest.
<LI> Personal hygiene for subjects includes showers and sponge baths, performed as needed, with the assistance of the nursing staff.</LI></OL><P>

This study integrated the requirements of four different principal investigators in the FAP 30 Day Head Down Tilt bed rest model.  Each of the four investigator had a different subject requirement; therefore, not all subjects enrolled in the study received the complete complement from the investigators science tests.  As each investigator completed their subject requirement, the testing associated with that study was removed from the integrated testing complement.  
<P>
<i>Phase A Synopsis</i><br>
Phase A, began on October 13, 2009, and ended on December 24, 2009.  Subjects participated in 13 days of the pre-bed rest phase, 30 days of in-bed rest, and 14 days of post-bed rest phase.  A total of three subjects were admitted into the study; of these, all three completed the study successfully.  This phase started the collection of Standard Measures and included investigator science tests for Thermoregulation, Calcium Isotope, Near Infrared Spectroscopy (NIRS), and Smart Pill.<P>

<i>Phase B Synopsis</I><br>
Phase B had the same numbers of study days as described for Phase A.  Phase B began on November 3, 2009, and ended on December 31, 2009.  A total of two subjects were admitted into the study and both subjects completed the study.  This phase continued the ongoing collection of Standard Measures and included investigator science test of Calcium Isotope, NIRS, and Smart Pill.<p>

<i>Phase C Synopsis</I><br>
Phase C began on November 21, 2009, and ended on February 28, 2010.  Subjects participated in 13 days of the pre-bed rest phase, 30 days of the in-bed rest phase, and 14 days of the post-bed rest phase.  A total of four subjects were admitted and three subjects successfully completed the study and one subject was dismissed from the study.  This phase continued the ongoing collation of Standard Measures and added the sublingual scrape to the Clinical Nutritional Assessment (MR016G) and increased blood volume requirements.  This phase included investigator science tests for Calcium Isotope, NIRS, Smart Pill, and Thermoregulation. 
<p>
<i>Phase D Synopsis</I><br>
Phase D had the same number of study days as described in previous phases.  This phase began on January 19, 2010, and ended on May 13, 2010.  A total of four subjects were admitted to the study and all four subjects completed this phase of the study. This phase continued the ongoing collection of Standard measures and included investigator science test for Calcium Isotope and NIRS for all fours subjects.<p>
In addition to the Standard Measures testing, four extramural experiments were conducted during this study which included :<P>
 <LI> Non-Invasive Near Infrared Spectroscopy (NIRS)<BR> (PI: Soller, Babs R., Ph.D., University of Massachusetts)<P> 
<LI> Rapid Measurements of Bone Loss Using Tracer-Less Calcium Isotope Analysis of Blood and Urine (Calcium_Isotope)<BR> (PI: Anbar, Ariel, Ph.D., Arizona State University)<P>
 <LI>Thermoregulation (Thermo)<BR> (PI: Gunga, Hanns-Christian, Ph.D., University of Berlin)<P>
<LI>Methods for the Assessment of Gastrointestinal Physiology and Function in a Lunar Analog Environment (SmartPill)<BR> (PI: Putcha, Lak, Ph.D., NASA Johnson Space Center (JSC)) </LI><P><end>
